<article>
  <div class="article-header">
    <h1>Cannabis Topicals &amp; CBD Skin Products — Maine Guide</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Balms, salves, lotions, transdermal patches: how cannabis topicals work in Maine</p>
  </div>

  <div class="verification">
    <strong>Last verified:</strong> July 4, 2026. Maine statute citations confirmed against the Title 28-B statutory text (<a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/28-b/title28-Bsec701.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§701</a>, <a href="https://legislature.maine.gov/statutes/28-b/title28-Bsec703.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">§703</a>) and OCP guidance. Title 28-B §703(1)(F)'s 10mg-per-serving THC cap applies specifically to <em>edible</em> cannabis products; topicals are not subject to that cap. Always verify topical ingredients and mg-per-container with the operator and request the batch COA before use.
  </div>

  <section class="fact-box">
    <h2>Cannabis Topicals at a Glance</h2>
    <table aria-label="Cannabis topical product key facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Attribute</th><th scope="col">Detail</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Product types</strong></td><td>Balms, salves, lotions, creams, massage oils, bath salts, transdermal patches, intimate lubricants</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Common carriers</strong></td><td>Coconut oil, shea butter, beeswax, jojoba oil, aloe vera</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Maine per-serving THC cap</strong></td><td>None (topicals are not "edibles" under Title 28-B §703(1)(F))</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Maine labeling requirements</strong></td><td>Title 28-B §701: ingredients, batch number, THC/CBD mg, route of administration, expiration</td></tr>
